  "related_work": "Li, Luo, Wang, and Feng motivate quantile-targeted response adaptation and fixed-horizon efficiency for a selected QTE; their result informs the local allocation problem but is not claimed to be extended. Dai, Gradu, and Harshaw and Noarov, Fogliato, Bertran, and Roth develop scalar ATE Neyman regret, which informs the online loss analysis. Howard and Ramdas derive i.i.d. quantile confidence sequences, while Mineiro and Howard construct time- and value-uniform CDF bands under nonstationarity; both inform the inversion construction. Zhang and Zheng establish fixed-horizon QTE-process asymptotics under covariate-adaptive randomization and inform the regularity comparison. Simchi-Levi and Wang formulate a welfare-versus-mean-inference Pareto problem and inform the frontier viewpoint. Cook, Mishler, and Ramdas and Hadad, Hirshberg, Zhan, Wager, and Athey inform predictable martingale weighting for adaptive experiments. None of these papers is presented as a theorem-level result automatically sharpened by the statements here.",
  "technical_internal_limitation": "The regular core excludes atoms and vanishing-density cusps. The explicit coverage band is intentionally conservative and need not attain the frontier; local empirical-Bernstein calibration and allocation learning belong to the open attainment problem.",
